\section{Beyond the assumptions: optimiser, target, depth and a ViT}
\label{sec:ext}

The race assumes gradient descent, a one-layer linear encoder, and a collapse decided near the collapsed point.
We relax each assumption in turn and ask whether the mechanism survives, and in what form.
Unless marked retrospective or post hoc, each prediction below was frozen before its held-out runs and scored once, against baselines that include the static bound and, in the linear models, the count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive} ($\mu$ count, equal to the initial window in the aligned linear model); \cref{app:ext} gives the derivations and every criterion (\cref{tab:ext-tests}).
A model of weight-decay schedules, which change the decay after capture, follows the alive state of held-out runs closely but fails its registered test on short pulses and on the predictor after a loss (\cref{sec:sched}).

